WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.230
Lab reports in India are messy.

2
00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:05.800
Different names, different units, different layouts.

3
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.430
In Round 1, we built LabLens.

4
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.530
It flags abnormal results, and the combinations that need a doctor.

5
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.830
But it could only check what it could read.

6
00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.720
So for Round 2, we trained our own model.

7
00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.460
LabLens Reader. Trained with Adaption AutoScientist.

8
00:00:21.697 --> 00:00:26.947
Three thousand training examples, in English, Hindi and Marathi.

9
00:00:27.177 --> 00:00:31.007
Our rules engine wrote every answer, so every label is correct.

10
00:00:32.107 --> 00:00:35.377
AutoScientist tried different training settings, and kept the best.

11
00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:39.067
Our model beat the original in 59% of head-to-head comparisons.

12
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:41.837
And a second run found the exact same recipe.

13
00:00:42.157 --> 00:00:44.747
Now, a doctor's note it never saw in training.

14
00:00:44.987 --> 00:00:47.817
The original model says, discuss with your doctor.

15
00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:51.857
Ours says, see a doctor today. And the rules engine agrees.

16
00:00:52.147 --> 00:00:57.517
A PDF dump in Marathi: all 23 values, read correctly.

17
00:00:57.797 --> 00:01:03.597
On 99 reports it had never seen, fully correct answers went from 3% to 55%.

18
00:01:03.977 --> 00:01:07.367
Missed urgent cases fell from 51% to 4%.

19
00:01:07.497 --> 00:01:09.467
And the AI never gets the last word.

20
00:01:09.657 --> 00:01:11.527
Our rules engine checks every value.

21
00:01:11.777 --> 00:01:15.687
LabLens. Built for ORCATHON 2026.

